//loop

A loop allows a program to execute a block of statements repeatedly.

C provides three main types of loops:
- while
- for
- do-while

//Continuously accepting clients
//Socket servers commonly use an infinite loop because they continuously wait for connections.
while (1) {
    /* Wait for a client */
}



for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
}


while (condition) {
    // Statements
}


#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 1;

    while (i <= 5) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
        i++;
    }

    return 0;
}

i++ increments i by 1. It is equivalent to i = i + 1.


for (initialization; condition; update) {
    // Statements
}


#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }

    return 0;
}


A do-while loop executes the statements at least once, because the condition is evaluated after the loop body.

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 1;

    do {
        printf("%d\n", i);
        i++;
    } while (i <= 5);

    return 0;
}


break and continue
- break terminates the nearest enclosing loop immediately.
- continue skips the remaining statements in the current iteration and proceeds to the next iteration.

